1 OPP
  opp, the Oberon pretty printer, reformats Oberon-2 modules written for
  poc (the Peaseblossom Oberon Compiler) or voc (Vishap Oberon). It keeps
  every comment and the author's blank lines, lays the code out within a
  maximum line length, and puts statements one per line, packs short ones
  onto shared lines, or keeps the author's own grouping. Before it writes
  anything for a file, it checks that the formatted text has exactly the
  file's tokens and comments, and parses.

  OPP.EXE takes its arguments as a foreign command:

    $ OPP :== $DISK$USER:[TOOLS]OPP.EXE

  Format:

    OPP [options] [file ...]

  DCL turns what is not quoted into capitals, and opp lowercases every
  argument that starts with "-", so options may be typed in either case.
2 Parameters
  file ...

  The modules to format. A name may have the wildcards *, % and ...,
  which opp expands itself; a pattern that matches no file is reported,
  with exit status 1. With no file, or "-" (quoted, since a "-" at the
  end of a DCL line continues the line), opp reads SYS$INPUT.

  Each formatted file goes to SYS$OUTPUT, unless --in-place, --output-dir
  or --check is given. The text after the period that ends a module is
  copied as it is.
2 Options
  -w n, --width n       the maximum line length (20 to 1000; default 80)
  -i n, --indent n      columns per indentation level (1 to 16; default 2)
  -1, --one-per-line    every statement on its own line (the default)
  -p, --pack            pack short statements and declarations onto
                        shared lines
  -k, --keep-lines      keep statements and declarations on shared lines
                        where the source has them, while they fit
  --no-module-indent    start a module's declarations at column 1
  --module-indent       indent a module's declarations (the default)
  --case-bar what       a bar before a CASE's first arm: keep (the
                        default), add or drop
  --paren-space what    a blank before the parenthesis of a call, a type
                        guard or a procedure's parameters, as P (x):
                        keep (the default), add or drop
  --cuddle-begin what   a body's first statement on BEGIN's line: allow
                        (the default), force or disallow
  --cuddle-end what     END on that line too: allow, force or disallow
  --align words         what to line up, words separated by commas:
                        const, type, var, field, init, comment, case,
                        assign or all; no before one (nocomment) for one
                        space; keep (the default) or drop for the
                        author's spaces elsewhere
  --in-place            replace each file with its formatted text
  --output-dir dir      write each file's formatted text to dir
  -c, --check           write nothing; name each file that would change,
                        and exit 1 if there is one
  --tokens              list each file's tokens and comments instead
  --config file         read options from file, not the config files
  --no-config           read no config file
  -h, --help            show the usage and exit

  When more than one of -1, -p and -k is given, the last one wins.
2 In_place
  --in-place writes the formatted text as a new version of each file,
  Stream_LF, and leaves the version it read as it was: that version is
  the backup, and PURGE removes it. The new version has the old one's
  protection. A file that is already formatted, or has a syntax error,
  gets no new version. A file of any record format can be formatted,
  including the variable-length records that EDT and FTP write. A file
  named with a version, as "LEXER.MOD;3", is read from that version, and
  the new version goes above the highest.
2 Output_dir
  --output-dir dir writes each file's formatted text to dir, a directory
  that must exist, as [.FORMATTED] or DUA1:[USERS.ME.OUT], under the
  file's name and type, as a new version if there is one already. Every
  file is written, even one already formatted, and none for a file with
  a syntax error. Two files of the same name are a mistake, found before
  anything is written, and a file is not written over itself.
2 Config_files
  Before the command line, opp reads options from the user's config file,
  the one the logical name OPP$CONFIG names, or else SYS$LOGIN:OPP.CONF,
  and then from OPP.CONF in the current directory. A later setting wins
  over an earlier one, and the command line over both; either file may be
  missing.

  Each line holds one option by its long name, with or without its --, and
  its value after white space or =. A flag is its name alone. # starts a
  comment. Only options that say how to format may be in a config file.
  For example:

    width = 100
    pack
    no-module-indent
    align all,nocomment

  A mistake in a config file is reported as file:line: message, with exit
  status 2.
2 Exit_status
  opp's status is a condition value in $STATUS: SS$_NORMAL for success,
  and for a status n an error, %X10000000 + 8 * n + 2, whose message DCL
  does not print. A procedure recovers n as ($STATUS .AND. %XFFF8) / 8.

    1  %X1000000A  a syntax error or a file that cannot be read or
                   written, or with --check a file that is not formatted
    2  %X10000012  a mistake on the command line or in a config file
    3  %X1000001A  an internal error: a formatted text failed the check,
                   and was not written

  A syntax error is reported as file:line:column: message.
2 Restrictions
  SYS$OUTPUT is written a record at a time, and a record holds at most
  1024 characters, so a longer line, which only a long comment or string
  can make, is broken in two there; --in-place and --output-dir are not
  affected. opp counts widths as UTF-8 text, in which the bytes A0 to BF
  hexadecimal take no column, so a line with those characters of the DEC
  Multinational Character Set can come out wider than the width.
2 Examples
  Format a module to SYS$OUTPUT:

    $ OPP LEXER.MOD

  Format modules in place, packing short statements, at width 100:

    $ OPP -P -W 100 --IN-PLACE *.MOD

  Write formatted copies of the modules to a subdirectory:

    $ OPP --OUTPUT-DIR [.FORMATTED] *.MOD

  List the modules that are not formatted as the author grouped them:

    $ OPP -K --CHECK *.MOD
